  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero information about which design choices matter. The most critical uncertainty is the overall performance level of a standard multimodal pipeline and whether combining RNA and image modalities is viable at all. 2) What this tests: config_006 (multimodal, multiprofile, scgpt, resnet50, avgpool, concat) is a 'central' configuration using the most established and commonly expected choices in each dimension: scgpt as a well-validated RNA encoder, resnet50 as a standard pretrained image encoder, avgpool as the simplest aggregation, and concat as the simplest fusion. It establishes a performance anchor for the multimodal approach. 3) Why it is the best first use of budget: Starting from a standard, well-motivated multimodal baseline lets every subsequent experiment isolate a single design change (e.g., swapping avgpool→transformer, concat→weighted, resnet50→ViT-L, scgpt→scvi, or dropping a modality for a single-modality baseline). This maximizes the information gained per experiment under a limited budget, because the reference point is meaningful and comparable across many future variants. A single-modality baseline would be informative but would not directly test the core multimodal hypothesis that motivates this study."
}

